---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PromptPage from "../../../components/PromptPage.astro";

const masterPrompt = `Act as an experienced competitive intelligence researcher.

TASK:
Research the competitor described below and build an evidence-based competitor research profile.

This is a RESEARCH task. Focus on finding, organizing and evaluating verifiable information rather than automatically recommending business strategy.

COMPETITOR:
[Company, brand, product or service.]

WEBSITE:
[Official website if known.]

MARKET / LOCATION:
[Country, region, city or online market.]

INDUSTRY:
[Industry or business category.]

MY RESEARCH GOAL:
[What do you want to understand about this competitor?]

MY BUSINESS / PRODUCT:
[Optional — describe your own business if relevant.]

TARGET CUSTOMER:
[Customer group relevant to the research.]

RESEARCH PERIOD:
[Current / last 12 months / historical period / specific dates.]

KNOWN INFORMATION:
[List anything already known.]

SPECIFIC QUESTIONS:
[List any questions you particularly want answered.]

RESEARCH DEPTH:
[Quick / Standard / Deep]

COMPETITOR RESEARCH REQUIREMENTS:

1. IDENTIFY THE CORRECT COMPETITOR
Verify:
- official company / brand name
- official website
- primary market
- main business activity
- relevant product or service

Watch for companies with similar names.

Do not combine information from different businesses.

2. COMPANY OVERVIEW
Research where available:
- company description
- location
- markets served
- founding information
- ownership
- company size indicators
- business model
- major customer segments

Clearly mark information that cannot be verified.

3. PRODUCTS & SERVICES
Identify:
- main products
- services
- packages
- product categories
- service tiers
- major features
- add-ons
- delivery model

Use current official information where possible.

4. TARGET CUSTOMERS
Investigate who the competitor appears to serve.

Look for evidence from:
- website messaging
- product design
- pricing
- case studies
- testimonials
- industries served
- advertising
- distribution channels

Separate explicit statements from inference.

5. VALUE PROPOSITION
Identify:
- main customer problem addressed
- promised benefit
- key selling points
- reasons customers are given to choose the competitor

Use actual evidence rather than generic assumptions about the industry.

6. POSITIONING
Investigate how the competitor positions itself.

Possible positioning dimensions include:
- low cost
- premium
- specialist
- simple
- fast
- convenient
- innovative
- local
- enterprise
- personalized
- comprehensive

Explain the evidence supporting the classification.

7. PRICING
Research where available:
- listed prices
- subscription plans
- service packages
- free plans
- trials
- discounts
- bundles
- minimum orders
- setup fees
- recurring charges

Record:
- currency
- date checked
- source

If current pricing cannot be verified, state:
"Pricing not verified."

Do not invent prices.

8. SALES CHANNELS
Identify how customers can purchase or access the offer.

Examples:
- direct website
- physical stores
- sales representatives
- marketplaces
- distributors
- resellers
- mobile app
- social commerce

9. MARKETING CHANNELS
Investigate visible use of:
- search
- social media
- email
- content marketing
- video
- influencers
- paid advertising
- partnerships
- events
- marketplaces
- affiliate programs

Do not assume a channel is important merely because an account exists.

10. MESSAGING
Identify recurring themes in:
- headlines
- calls to action
- product descriptions
- advertisements
- social posts
- landing pages

Summarize the messaging rather than reproducing large amounts of copyrighted text.

11. CUSTOMER EVIDENCE
Research available:
- testimonials
- case studies
- ratings
- customer reviews
- community discussions
- recurring complaints
- recurring positive feedback

Distinguish:
- company-selected testimonials
- independent reviews
- informal online comments

Do not treat isolated comments as representative.

12. PRODUCT / SERVICE EXPERIENCE
Where reliable evidence is available, investigate:
- ease of use
- ordering process
- onboarding
- delivery
- customer support
- returns
- cancellation
- implementation
- usability

Clearly separate direct evidence from inference.

13. REPUTATION
Look for credible evidence concerning:
- brand reputation
- customer satisfaction
- service issues
- major complaints
- awards
- certifications
- regulatory issues
- public controversies

Avoid amplifying unverified allegations.

14. BUSINESS MODEL
Determine how the competitor appears to make money.

Examples:
- one-time sale
- subscription
- service fee
- commission
- marketplace fee
- licensing
- advertising
- freemium
- consulting

If the model is inferred rather than confirmed, label it as an inference.

15. OPERATING MODEL
Where information is available, investigate:
- physical vs online operations
- locations
- delivery model
- fulfillment
- outsourcing
- partnerships
- distribution
- service process

Do not invent internal operational details.

16. TECHNOLOGY
Identify visible technology relevant to the offering.

Examples:
- mobile app
- customer portal
- automation
- integrations
- AI functionality
- e-commerce platform

Do not speculate about internal technology stacks without evidence.

17. PARTNERSHIPS & DISTRIBUTION
Identify publicly documented:
- strategic partnerships
- suppliers
- distributors
- integrations
- channel partners
- marketplace relationships

18. RECENT DEVELOPMENTS
If current research is available, check for relevant recent:
- product launches
- pricing changes
- expansion
- acquisitions
- partnerships
- leadership changes
- funding
- closures
- regulatory developments

Use publication dates.

19. COMPETITOR STRENGTH SIGNALS
Identify evidence that may indicate strength, such as:
- broad distribution
- strong customer reviews
- established brand
- product depth
- partnerships
- customer retention signals
- scale
- specialization

Do not convert signals into unsupported claims.

20. WEAKNESS / FRICTION SIGNALS
Identify evidence of possible weaknesses such as:
- recurring complaints
- confusing pricing
- poor support
- missing functionality
- limited availability
- difficult onboarding
- delivery problems

Label these as signals unless sufficient evidence supports a stronger conclusion.

21. SOURCE STRATEGY
Prioritize:

Tier 1:
- official company website
- official product documentation
- official pricing
- financial filings
- regulators
- government databases

Tier 2:
- reputable news organizations
- established industry publications
- research organizations
- credible market reports

Tier 3:
- customer reviews
- forums
- community discussions
- social media

Use Tier 3 sources mainly for customer-experience signals rather than unquestioned factual claims.

22. SOURCE LOG
For important findings record:
- Finding
- Source
- Source type
- Publication / access date
- Primary or secondary
- Confidence level

23. CONFLICTING INFORMATION
When sources disagree:
- identify the conflict
- compare publication dates
- compare source authority
- identify which evidence is stronger
- state what remains uncertain

24. INFORMATION GAPS
Identify important information that could not be verified.

Do not fill gaps with assumptions.

25. RESEARCH CONFIDENCE
Classify major findings as:

HIGH CONFIDENCE
Supported by strong primary or multiple reliable sources.

MEDIUM CONFIDENCE
Reasonably supported but incomplete.

LOW CONFIDENCE
Based on limited, indirect or uncertain evidence.

UNVERIFIED
Insufficient evidence.

26. FURTHER RESEARCH
Recommend the highest-value next research actions.

Examples:
- request pricing
- test free trial
- conduct mystery shopping
- interview customers
- analyze recent reviews
- examine regulatory filings
- compare archived website versions
- monitor product changes

Use legal and ethical research methods.

OUTPUT FORMAT:

1. Research Summary
2. Competitor Identity
3. Company Overview
4. Products & Services
5. Target Customers
6. Value Proposition
7. Positioning
8. Pricing
9. Sales Channels
10. Marketing Channels
11. Messaging
12. Customer Evidence
13. Customer Experience
14. Reputation
15. Business Model
16. Operating Model
17. Technology
18. Partnerships & Distribution
19. Recent Developments
20. Strength Signals
21. Weakness / Friction Signals
22. Source Log
23. Conflicting Information
24. Information Gaps
25. Confidence Assessment
26. Further Research

IMPORTANT:
- Do not invent competitor information.
- Do not invent prices, revenue, customer numbers, market share, reviews or internal processes.
- Distinguish facts from inference.
- Clearly identify outdated information.
- Prefer current primary sources for factual company information.
- Use customer reviews as experience signals, not automatically as representative evidence.
- Do not treat promotional claims as independently verified facts.
- Do not make strategic recommendations unless specifically requested.
- The purpose is to produce a reliable competitor research profile based on evidence.`;

const steps = [
  {
    title: "Confirm the competitor first",
    description:
      "Verify the exact company, brand and official website before collecting information so similarly named businesses are not accidentally mixed together.",
  },
  {
    title: "Start with primary sources",
    description:
      "Use official product pages, pricing, documentation and company information to establish basic facts before adding third-party research.",
  },
  {
    title: "Add independent evidence",
    description:
      "Use credible news, industry sources and customer discussions to investigate reputation, customer experience and developments the company may not emphasize itself.",
  },
  {
    title: "Record uncertainty",
    description:
      "Separate confirmed information, reasonable inference and unverified claims instead of filling research gaps with assumptions.",
  },
];

const example = {
  input: [
    "Competitor: Inventory-management software provider.",
    "Research goal: Understand its offer before developing a service for small retailers.",
    "Questions: What customers does it target, what features and pricing does it offer, and what problems appear repeatedly in customer feedback?",
    "Research depth: Standard.",
  ],

  output: [
    "Begin by confirming the company's official website and current product offering.",
    "Use official product and pricing pages to document features, plans, target segments and stated value proposition.",
    "Use independent reviews and community discussions to identify recurring customer-experience themes, but separate those observations from verified product facts.",
    "Record each important finding with its source and date. Mark pricing, features or claims that cannot be verified as unverified rather than estimating them.",
    "Finish by identifying the most important remaining research gaps and the next evidence needed to understand the competitor more confidently.",
  ],
};

const tips = [
  {
    title: "Research before analyzing",
    description:
      "Collect reliable competitor evidence first. Strategic conclusions are stronger when they are based on verified information rather than assumptions.",
  },
  {
    title: "Separate company claims from evidence",
    description:
      "A competitor's website is excellent for understanding what it offers and how it positions itself, but promotional claims may still require independent verification.",
  },
  {
    title: "Date your findings",
    description:
      "Competitor pricing, features, messaging and partnerships can change quickly. Recording dates makes the research much easier to update later.",
  },
];

---

<BaseLayout
  title="Competitor Research Prompt | KRIYANO"
  description="Research a competitor systematically using verifiable evidence about products, pricing, customers, positioning, marketing, reviews, operations and recent developments."
>
  <PromptPage
    category="RESEARCH"
    title="Competitor Research"
    accentTitle="Prompt."
    description="Build an evidence-based competitor profile covering products, customers, pricing, positioning, channels, customer feedback and recent developments."
    meta={[
      "Free prompt",
      "Research",
      "Competitive intelligence",
    ]}
    bestFor={[
      "Competitor research",
      "Market research",
      "Business planning",
      "Product research",
      "Pricing research",
      "Competitive intelligence",
    ]}
    masterPrompt={masterPrompt}
    introTitle="Collect the evidence before drawing conclusions."
    introDescription="Research one competitor systematically using primary sources, independent evidence and customer signals while clearly separating confirmed facts from inference and unverified information."
    steps={steps}
    exampleTitle="Build a competitor profile from evidence."
    example={example}
    tipsTitle="Research competitors more reliably."
    tips={tips}
    sidebarTitle="Ready to compare competitors?"
    sidebarDescription="After collecting reliable competitor evidence, use the KRIYANO Competitor Analysis Prompt to compare positioning, strengths, risks, market gaps and differentiation opportunities."
    warningTitle="Competitor information changes"
    warningText="Products, pricing, features, leadership and market activity can change quickly. Record source dates and verify current information before relying on competitor research for important decisions."
  />
</BaseLayout>